.TH PY-ANNOTATE 1 "November 2026" "py-annotate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
py-annotate \- add annotations to PDF documents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B py-annotate
.RI [ file.pdf ]
.br
.B py-annotate
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B py-annotate
opens a window (Qt) in which annotations are added to a PDF document:
highlights, underlines and strikeouts over the text, marks where text is to be
inserted, notes, text boxes and callouts, highlighted regions, rectangles,
ellipses, polygons, clouds, lines, arrows, polylines, drawings and highlights
made by hand, and stamps. Each one can have a comment, replies, a review
status, tags and categories.
.PP
They are written as ordinary PDF annotations, which any PDF viewer shows and
can edit. The annotations a document already has are read, and can be changed
or deleted. Saving into the document adds the changes at its end (an
incremental save), so that its signatures stay valid for what they signed.
.PP
The window can also export the annotations (a report in Markdown, a table in
CSV, JSON, XFDF, a summary of the comments as a PDF), import them (JSON, XFDF,
another copy of the PDF), print the document, and save copies of it
flattened, without annotations, or as PDF/A or PDF/X (with
.BR pdf2iso ).
It is in English or Spanish (Settings > Language).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, and stop.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print how to start it, and stop.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/py-annotate/config.toml
The settings: the language, the author of new annotations, how each tool
draws, the colours and their meanings, the categories, the keyboard shortcuts
and where pdf2iso is.
.B $PY_ANNOTATE_CONFIG
names another folder.
.TP
.I ~/.config/py-annotate/recovery/
Copies of the annotations not yet saved, offered if the program stops before
they are saved.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pdf2iso (1)
